  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n- Showers And Storms Tuesday\n\nThe first round of showers is ongoing across Central and Northern \nMichigan associated with a 925-850mb FGEN axis across the Central \nGreat Lakes. This is area is expected to propagate southeast with \ntime across the area. Could be some heavy rainfall, however with \ntiming near diurnal minimum and associated poor instability severe \nweather is unlikely.\n\nThe next round tracks through this afternoon and evening with \ndevelopment ahead of a cold front. Given robust cloud cover and \nprecipitation as the front slides south, latest trends suggest \nmarginal instability will be in place here with better \ndestabilization south. However, there may be just enough surface \nbased instability, enhanced shear from the westerly flow, and PWATs \naoa 2\" to support a wet microburst threat south of I94. \n\n- Several additional chances of showers and storms this week.\n\nWe shift into a lower predictability pattern going into mid to late \nweek as predominantly zonal flow takes over. Right now the front \nlooks to stall south of us, but a northward shift enhances the risk \nfor several rounds of rainfall over the coming days. The exact \ncoverage/timing will be dependent on small perturbations in the large \nscale flow regime. Given continuing high PWATs in place across the \narea, periods of heavy rainfall are possible. The inverse is also \ntrue. Some solutions like the 15/00z GFS keep the front more \nsoutherly which would keep things drier. We then see another system \narrive late Friday into the weekend bringing the potential for \nanother round of showers and storms.",
